{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Bullish Breakouts: The model consistently fails to capture the magnitude of upward price spikes (e.g., the surge from ~80 to ~90 in late April), predicting only marginal increases. Correction: Apply a significant upward multiplier to forecasts when sentiment history shows consecutive high values (>= 0.67) combined with recent price volatility.",
    "Excessive Mean Reversion Bias: Forecasts exhibit a strong tendency to revert to the historical mean immediately after a price spike, ignoring momentum. Correction: Reduce the mean reversion speed in the model when true outcomes show sustained trends rather than immediate reversals.",
    "Sentiment Lag in Trend Identification: The model reacts to sentiment shifts with a delay, often predicting flat or declining prices while sentiment is already positive and prices are rising. Correction: Weight the most recent sentiment scores (last 3-5 days) more heavily than the rolling average to accelerate trend detection.",
    "Horizon-Specific Overshoot Correction: In the 15-20 day horizon, the model often predicts a peak followed by a decline, whereas true outcomes show a plateau or continued high volatility. Correction: Flatten the forecast curve in the mid-term horizon during periods of high sentiment to avoid premature downward projections.",
    "Failure to Price in Volatility Clusters: The model assumes smooth transitions between price levels, missing the sharp jumps characteristic of the observed data. Correction: Introduce a volatility-adjusted term that scales forecast variance based on the standard deviation of the last 10 days of history."
  ]
}
